  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2022-11-14T10:34:20.728Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "I know we already went from 128 to 256, but with the addition of OSC and people taking full advantage of it, even 256 bits is limiting for many advanced users. Especially with the fact that ALL parameters that need to be controlled by the expression menu have to be networked.\nI don't understand why there's even a limit this low when you consider players voices and IK movement must be so, so much more data in transmission. Or am I mistaken?\nPlease consider upping the amount of parameters we are allowed to use on avatars please. There have been many cases where I've had to straight up ditch an idea on my avatar despite it being within my medium/poor constraints because I don't have enough parameter space. Full face and eye tracking alone can easily use up to 150 bits. Want haptics too? Maybe a heart rate display? Not all happening at the same time without sacrifice.\nI have a HUD on my avatar that uses 32 bits for adjusting the opacity/size/depth and it doesn't even need to be networked, it's local only... There's just so many issues that seem heavily overlooked.\nI think a perfect system would be something along the lines of this:\n- More networked parameters, I think 512 would be way more than enough. It should be a limit nobody would need to hit, so nobody can complain. But still low enough to where it has no impact on players experiences. Could be limited to PC only too since most OSC features that could exceed 256 bits only work on PC anyway.\nBecause platform parameter sync is based on both the parameter names and order, it would be easy for creators to keep PC/Quest avatar sync despite a difference in parameter count across both platforms. \n- Parameter priority support, higher priority = faster sync\n- Possibly limit avatars based on network load rather than the amount of parameters they use. Rate of the lowest priority parameters can be auto limited. I don't see why someone can't have 10+ sliders just for avatar lighting when they become static values once used anyway.\nThey aren't updating in real time and dont need to sync fast when updated either. You could even create a new float type, realtime Vs static. Static only updates when the wheel is closed, then pushes values to the server at low intervals whereas realtime floats use IK sync. Again, for something like a brightness slider, a very low rate is needed just so late joiners can see the updated value.\n- Allow local parameters to be controlled by the expression menu\n- Slightly unrelated but, local only contacts that don't affect performance stats for OSC haptics, local only menu's on avatars and so on. Would be a perfect solution for people who use haptics in VR and have to constrain themselves in club worlds that have performance rank limits. It's very annoying that an avatar is deemed 'worse performance' for a feature most people set up to be local only with the isLocal parameter anyway. Just, why?\nHope this can be looked into further, thank you!",
